# The references global virtual memory holds into local virtual memory,
# and why each exists. See tests/check-global-local-anchors.sh for the
# rule and for how each row is held to the code.
#
# PLRM 3.7.2: a composite object in global VM may not reference local
# VM. The store-time barrier (xpost_dict_put, xpost_array_put) refuses
# such a store with an invalidaccess error. The PLRM itself carves out
# one exception: systemdict, a global dictionary, names several
# per-context local dictionaries so a program reaches each by name
# (PLRM 3.7.7). The interpreter opens the barrier
# (ctx->ignoreinvalidaccess) for exactly the routes below, and every
# one of them stores into systemdict alone.
#
# The collector depends on this enumeration. A collection of local vm
# alone marks the local roots and then anchors whatever systemdict
# holds in local vm (_xpost_garbage_mark_systemdict_exceptions in
# xpost_garbage.c) instead of walking the frozen global graph, whose
# objects a local sweep never touches. The anchor is derived from
# systemdict's slots, not from the names below, so a reference admitted
# through a sanctioned route is anchored without being enrolled here.
# What the rows hold to the code, in both directions, is the set of
# routes that may open the barrier and the set of names those routes
# store: a new route, or a new name stored by one, fails the check
# until it is entered here with its reason.
#
# route rows: where the barrier opens. Every ignoreinvalidaccess
# opening in src/lib must be inside a function named here, and every
# dictionary store such a function makes must target systemdict.
#
# where it is                            kind    what it stores
xpost_interpreter.c:copyudtosd           window  the local dictionaries systemdict names, put once at boot
xpost_op_save.c:_rebind_fontdirectory    window  FontDirectory retargeted between the local and the global font directory as the allocation mode changes
# table rows: storage in the global memory file that is not an object,
# holding references no container walk can reach. The collector walks
# each as a root in every collection, whichever bank is asked for, the
# walk stopping at what lies in a bank not in play. The wrapped
# accessors of per-context local state (.gscratch, graphicsdict,
# DEVICE) keep their procedures in local vm, so the operator table is a
# global-side holder of local references exactly as systemdict is.
#
# where it is                            kind    what it holds
xpost_operator.h:Xpost_Operator.proc     table   the procedure a wrapped operator runs, living in whichever bank built it
#
# name rows: the systemdict entries whose values are local. Each must
# survive a collection of local vm alone, reachable through systemdict
# and nothing else -- the boot file drops the userdict copies of these
# names, so systemdict is the only named route to them.
#
# name            kind    why it is local
userdict          name    the per-context current dictionary (PLRM 3.7.7); also rooted by the dictionary stack, which never pops below it
errordict         name    error handlers a program replaces are job state, isolated by save and restore
$error            name    the record of the last error, written by the error machinery as the job runs
FontDirectory     name    fonts defined in local vm are job state; the name follows the allocation mode to the global directory and back
statusdict        name    device and product status a program may change, so a job's changes are isolated by save and restore
serverdict        name    exitserver machinery a job enters and leaves; its state is the job's
